<article class="guide-content"><h1>A Financial Checklist Before Leaving a Job</h1>
<p class="byline">Econified · Updated September 5, 2026 · <a href="/how-it-works/">Methodology and corrections</a></p>

<p>A calculator can estimate an expense reserve. It cannot determine whether leaving a job is right. Identify what you know, what needs confirmation and how sensitive the budget is to delays.</p>
<h2>1. Estimate spending after employment ends</h2><p>Include housing, food, utilities, transport, insurance and required debt payments. Replace benefits that stop with actual quotes where possible. Add annual bills and one-time transition costs. Removing a commute may lower spending while replacing insurance raises it.</p>
<p>An example $3,000 monthly budget could include $1,400 housing, $500 food, $350 utilities and transport, $450 insurance and $300 debt. These illustrate a budget, not recommended limits or typical costs.</p>
<h2>2. Count available money</h2><p>Exclude tax reserves and committed funds. Use net severance only when availability is confirmed. Do not assume unemployment benefits apply after voluntary resignation; check eligibility and payment dates with the responsible agency.</p>
<p>At $3,000 monthly spending, $18,000 cash gives six months without income. A $3,000 one-time bill reduces that to five months. Reducing ongoing spending to $2,500 extends the remaining $15,000 to six months.</p>
<h2>3. Test payment delays</h2><p>Adding future benefits to today's savings can overstate runway. With $1,000 cash, $3,000 monthly spending and $1,000 monthly benefits, the shortfall is $2,000 a month. Cash lasts about half a month even if benefits are expected for longer.</p>
<p>The <a href="/layoff-survival-calculator/">layoff runway calculator</a> accounts for benefit duration but assumes payments start immediately. A waiting period needs a separate check against your bill calendar.</p>
<h2>4. Choose a reserve and calculate the gap</h2><p>Six months is an example target, not proof of readiness. Dependents, irregular costs and uncertainty can change the reserve you choose. The <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/an-essential-guide-to-building-an-emergency-fund/">CFPB explains why emergency-fund needs depend on individual circumstances</a>.</p>
<p>If the target is $18,000 and you have $10,000, the gap is $8,000. Saving $1,000 monthly at zero return closes it in eight months. At $1,500 it takes about 5.3 months in a continuous approximation, so six full monthly deposits reach the target.</p>
<h2>5. Make uncertainty visible</h2><p>Run higher expenses, remove unconfirmed income and consider a delayed start for the next role. Use the <a href="/quit-calculator/">savings target calculator</a> for accumulation. Keep employment prospects, health, family needs and departure terms alongside the numbers.</p>

